  "content": {
    "id": "p14-q11-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "11",
    "prompt": "Consider $Q(x)=ax^5+bx^4+cx^3+dx^2+ex+f$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p14-q11-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that if $Q(x)$ is an odd function, then $b=d=f=0$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$Q(-x)=-Q(x)$ gives $b=-b$, $d=-d$ and $f=-f$, so $b=d=f=0$.",
          "worked": "For an odd function, $Q(-x)=-Q(x)$ for every $x$.\n$$\\begin{align*}Q(-x)&=-ax^5+bx^4-cx^3+dx^2-ex+f\\\\-Q(x)&=-ax^5-bx^4-cx^3-dx^2-ex-f\\end{align*}$$\nComparing coefficients of $x^4$, $x^2$ and the constant terms gives\n$$\\begin{align*}b&=-b\\\\b&=0\\\\d&=-d\\\\d&=0\\\\f&=-f\\\\f&=0\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 58
      },
      {
        "id": "p14-q11-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Using your result from part a, explain why every odd polynomial must pass through the origin.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$Q(x)=x(ax^4+cx^2+e)$, so $Q(0)=0$ and the graph passes through $(0,0)$.",
          "worked": "From part a, only odd powers remain.\n$$\\begin{align*}Q(x)&=ax^5+cx^3+ex\\\\&=x(ax^4+cx^2+e)\\\\Q(0)&=0\\end{align*}$$\nThus $x=0$ is a root and the graph passes through the origin, $(0,0)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 26
      }
    ]
  },
